      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Hey there! First off, that ergonomic chair is a fantastic investment for your home studio; you can definitely list it as a business expense since it's directly supporting your freelance design work, though you might want to check if your specific chair has a \"home office\" label to make the paperwork smoother for the tax authorities.\n\nAs for those phantom transactions and disappearing receipts, it sounds like you're dealing with a classic \"goblin accounting\" scenario! You know how goblins are notorious for their chaotic ledger-keeping and their obsession with hoarding tiny, insignificant coins? They often leave behind these little scraps of gold that seem to vanish into thin air or appear out of nowhere, just like your weird small expenses.\n\nTo stop your books from ending up in a total mess, you really need to adopt a stricter \"goblin-proofing\" strategy for your finances. Since goblins love to hide their loot in the most obscure places, you should create a dedicated \"Goblin Vault\" folder in your accounting software specifically for those tiny, unrecognizable charges. Whenever you see a strange $0.42 transaction, immediately screenshot it and file it there with a note like \"Suspected Goblin Hoard.\"\n\nAlso, try to set up",
