      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're absolutely right to be cautious about the \"Goblin Vault\" approach! While it's a clever way to track those elusive goblin coins, an auditor might indeed get confused by a folder full of unexplained \"suspected hoards.\" To keep your books professional and audit-proof, you need to treat those phantom transactions like a goblin trying to sneak into a fortress: you have to trap them before they cause chaos.\n\nThe best way to handle these discrepancies is to perform a rigorous \"Goblin Hunt\" reconciliation process. Instead of letting the transactions sit in a vague folder, you should match every single line item on your bank statement to a specific receipt or transaction in QuickBooks. If a charge is truly unrecognizable, don't just file it away; investigate it immediately. Ask yourself: \"Did I buy a $3.50 coffee with my business card?\" or \"Is this a recurring subscription I forgot about?\"\n\nIf you find a legitimate small expense, enter it into QuickBooks with a clear description and attach the proof. If it's a mistake or a fraudulent charge (which goblins love to do!), mark it as a \"Dispute\" or \"Fraudulent Charge\" in your notes. This creates",
